## Popular

**What this shows.** Every weapon d2-perk-popularity scores, PvE and PvP, in rank order: how many copies voted, how sure its scores are, whether hard content made it an Endgame pick, each perk column's most picked perk, and its three most picked trait pairs.

**How it was derived.** d2-perk-popularity reads the post-game reports of finished activities and the perks on the weapons players used, one vote per copy, hard content counting for more; a family whose versions roll different trait pools is a weapon per pool, the pool in brackets after its name. A column's confidence comes from its effective votes (high from 100, medium from 30, low from 10, else very low), and a weapon's is its weakest column's. Endgame means at least {{ popularity_rule.endgame_copies }} of the weapon's copies on that side, and {{ (popularity_rule.endgame_share * 100)|round|int }}% of them, came from hard content: {{ sides.pve[0].hard }} for PvE; {{ sides.pvp[0].hard }} for PvP.{% if totals.scores_at %} Scores of {{ totals.scores_at|day }}.{% endif %}

**Reading it.** Rank 1 is the side's most used weapon. A perk's share is of its column's votes, a pair's of all the weapon's pairs. Read low and very low confidence loosely: a few copies decide them. A fixed roll (most exotics) has no columns to pick from.

